ety and his selfishness  both of these things don't necessarily  make him a bad person  but simply an inexperienced and  overwhelmed child  nonetheless based on these weaknesses  gone's character  is set up to learn about the complex  moral fabric of society  and form a more realistic view of the  world this need is also moral  since through his basic moral compass  and his nativity  combined with his enormous power he  starts to hurt people around him  however it is not until the chimera and  arc  that gon completely spirals into a  complete loss of his morality  and ultimately his humanity there are  two events in particular towards the  beginning of the arc  that are representative for this change  the first is gone's first  ever kill in the story a low-ranked  armadillo chimera ant  that bluntly attacks him and had gone  then slowly crushes to death  while it is definitely understandable  that gon defends himself  and maybe even has to kill the chimera  and the complete  lack of reaction and emotional response  to this act  is what is truly shocking here the pure  gone we got to know at the beginning of  the story  would never have been able to do such a  thing at least not without being deeply  affected by it  and yet here gone has already been so  consumed by power and violence  that not only is he unshaken by his  first kill but he doesn't even give it a  second thought  and even more impactful for his  character is the death of his mentor  kite  who is killed by the royal guard pitot  this first ever loss of a loved person  affects gone deeply way more than is  common for typical shonen characters  much rather gone has a very realistic  and human response to this event  and develops a rage and thirst for  revenge against pito  that is honestly quite unsettling to  watch  as a result his final act in the arc is  one of the most notorious events in  shonen manga  he uses the heavily injured and innocent  komugi  as a mere tool to blackmail pitou giving  no sec