ety and his selfishness  both of these things don't necessarily  make him a bad person  but simply an inexperienced and  overwhelmed child  nonetheless based on these weaknesses  gone's character  is set up to learn about the complex  moral fabric of society  and form a more realistic view of the  world this need is also moral  since through his basic moral compass  and his nativity  combined with his enormous power he  starts to hurt people around him  however it is not until the chimera and  arc  that gon completely spirals into a  complete loss of his morality  and ultimately his humanity there are  two events in particular towards the  beginning of the arc  that are representative for this change  the first is gone's first  ever kill in the story a low-ranked  armadillo chimera ant  that bluntly attacks him and had gone  then slowly crushes to death  while it is definitely understandable  that gon defends himself  and maybe even has to kill the chimera  and the complete  lack of reaction and emotional response  to this act  is what is truly shocking here the pure  gone we got to know at the beginning of  the story  would never have been able to do such a  thing at least not without being deeply  affected by it  and yet here gone has already been so  consumed by power and violence  that not only is he unshaken by his  first kill but he doesn't even give it a  second thought  and even more impactful for his  character is the death of his mentor  kite  who is killed by the royal guard pitot  this first ever loss of a loved person  affects gone deeply way more than is  common for typical shonen characters  much rather gone has a very realistic  and human response to this event  and develops a rage and thirst for  revenge against pito  that is honestly quite unsettling to  watch  as a result his final act in the arc is  one of the most notorious events in  shonen manga  he uses the heavily injured and innocent  komugi  as a mere tool to blackmail pitou giving  no second thought about the overall  situation  and threatening to kill her once he  learns that kite is beyond help  in order to satisfy his wrath against  pitot he calls upon  all of the potential energy he will ever  have for a short bout of combat  where he brutally kills his opponent  without a single shred of remorse  in doing so he trades the rest of his  life  for one short moment of violence as the  horrified kiluah  is unable to do anything but watch it's  some of the most harrowing reading of  watching in the genre  a perverse version of goku's spirit bomb  this is gone's moment of moral choice  but instead of having a self-revelation  about what it means to be human  and how to behave justly and morally  towards others in the situation  he gives in to his worst instincts as  kilua abandons his merciless past by  forging a relationship with gone  his friend sacrifices all of his  remaining humanity  for the ultimate act of revenge and  becoming a monster in the process  the self-revelation meant forgone in  this moment of extreme moral and human  complexity  is so obvious that we the readers have  it instead  and it makes the scene just the more  dark and uncomfortable to watch  the reason why this dark twist on the  typical shonen protagonist arc  works so well with gone is because  togashi has followed one of the most  important rules of writing when creating  his character  creating empathy but not sympathy  creating sympathy for a character means  that we approve of the hero's actions  and completely identify with him as a  person if that were to be true however  we could never have morally challenging  and interesting main characters  and every single story would only serve  a very limited and specific audience  that shares the exact same views and  values as the hero  empathy on the other hand means that  while we don't necessarily approve of  the heroes actions  we do however understand them we  understand why the hero would make this  decision  and how he ultimately reached that moral  conclusion  and as you might suspect foreshadowing  plays a crucial role in this  it makes the story cohesive and engaging  so  when we apply this to gone while his  brutal murder of pito  and his failure to keep his humanity are  devastating to watch  we do fully understand why gon is  behaving this way  and does feel like we're watching the  natural climax to his character arc  his entire journey has slowly built  towards this dramatic moment  and we could unconsciously witness gon's  creeping moral decline  without togashi having to throw it into  our faces  this is truly great writing  it is a great endpoint to the story  where gon ultimately  meets his father and is judged worthy of  traveling with him  in a twisted way he has managed to  fulfill his desire  and going back to the traditional tropes  of shonen togashi establishes a new  equilibrium here  and as the author stated himself at this  point in hunter hunter  where gon meets jing he has pretty much  already completed the story once now you  may rightfully say  doesn't hunter hunter keep going  afterwards and of course  you're right however as you might know  if you follow the manga  gone is not really currently part of the  story and should he ever appear in it  again in the form of a main character  he will need a completely fresh arc with  a new desire line  and now possibly a positive character  arc where he  re-evaluates his past actions and  ultimately regains his humanity  ending up better and wiser than at the  beginning of the story  the one who enabled gon second chance  and survival meanwhile  was his best friend kirua who started  off as the cold-blooded and inhuman  assassin at the beginning of the story  who has now completely changed places  with gone  in many ways kilua is written just as  fantastically as gon  and we'll be looking at his character  and role in the story in the next video  of the series  so stay tuned for that thanks guys peace
 

